hls: subtitles in playlists of their own, read when wanted and in time

A master playlist names its subtitles as renditions (EXT-X-MEDIA,
TYPE=SUBTITLES), each a playlist of WebVTT segments. The reader made a
stream for each and read its segments once the stream was wanted. This
is what stood between that and subtitles a player can show.

Every one of those playlists was fetched while the media was opened,
one request after the other, before the first packet: a programme with
eight languages of subtitles opened eight requests later, whether
anyone wanted subtitles or not. What the stream is, is known without
the playlist. It is fetched when its stream is first wanted; one that
will not come is asked for again two seconds later and costs nothing
but its subtitles.

Each WebVTT segment says, in an X-TIMESTAMP-MAP, which timestamp of the
other segments its cue times stand for (RFC 8216, 3.5). The WebVTT
reader passed the header over, so that cues of a programme whose
timestamps begin at ten seconds, as they commonly do, came ten seconds
early. The reader has an option, timestamp_map, which the HLS reader
sets for its segments: the cue times are moved by what the header says.
Without a header they are the timestamps themselves, as the RFC has
it. A timestamp of 33 bits starts over every twenty-six and a half
hours, and the cue times with it, while the other streams are carried
on past that by their demuxers: a cue is put within half a round of
what was last read.

A playlist wanted from now on started at the time last read, which is
ahead of the time shown by all that waits to be played: the cue that is
up at that moment and those of the next seconds were never seen. The
caller says what is shown (subtitles_from, in microseconds), and the
playlist starts there, also one that is wanted still and is to be read
again. A cue that began before the time asked for and is still up at it
is passed on, after a seek too: it is judged by its end.

The reader took the first cue it read for where the subtitles'
playlist begins in time, and after a seek asked for cues that much
later than the time sought: with a first cue five seconds into the
film, the cues of the five seconds after every seek were let go of. A
cue says nothing of where its playlist begins, nor of how far the
reading has got: the time last read is not taken from one either, and
neither is the number of the segment the other playlists are at.

A cue that is still up when its segment ends is in the next segment
too. It was passed on twice, and the second time with a timestamp that
goes backwards. It is passed on once.

A segment that could not be had, or a playlist that could not be
fetched again, was an error of the whole media. It costs its cues.

The segments were read on end until one held a cue: through a quarter
of an hour in which nobody speaks, a hundred and fifty requests with
nothing else read meanwhile. A segment of a list that is complete is
read when the other playlists have been read to where it begins. Each
was fetched over a connection of its own; the connection is kept for
the next where connections are kept, as the other playlists keep theirs.

Waiting at the end of a live list, the playlist counted every packet
of the other playlists as a fetch that had brought nothing new, and
after m3u8_hold_counters of them, a quarter of a minute of a channel,
was taken to have ended for good. And every segment was read through a
buffer of its own that nothing let go of, 32 kB a segment.

